commit 3955f8304b41cc958cd700b7ae5ebd7b83b0fba2
Author: María <mariagrandury@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Sep 22 23:05:39 2026 +0200

    data: add the DCLMP and FWEB English corpora as the L=1 edu-filter axis
    
    L=1 has no language axis, so its only family contrast was depth — one pair,
    where RULES.md rule 5 needs three. Two schemes now vary the EDUCATIONAL-QUALITY
    FILTER of the English half instead: DCLMP reads dclm_processed (the same
    pipeline minus the edu classifier, flat, ~3,241B tokens) and FWEB reads plain
    FineWeb restricted to crawls <= 2022 — DCLM's own Common Crawl window, read one
    file per crawl in rotation so a deterministic prefix walk does not spend the
    whole budget inside CC-MAIN-2013-20. Both are L=1, deep only, seed 1904,
    90M..1.7B, 184.0B target: 12 cells, ~2,070 node-h if the training decision goes
    ahead. Registered and building; nothing is launched.
    
    The analysis needed an axis for this, and did not have one. DESIGN_AXES mapped
    every scheme's English to the same level, so the three deep L=1 families
    collapsed onto one row: the pairs that actually vary the corpus differed on
    NOTHING and were filed multi-axis, while the mono-axis set filled with three
    (x-deep, shallow) pairs — the depth decision reported three times as three
    design pairs, which is worse than the blank L=1 gives today. utils.py gains an
    `en` axis and scale_convergence its AXIS_LABEL entry, without which the axis is
    dropped silently. Additive: over the 38 real families the multi/mono/seed pair
    sets are unchanged at 276/62/29.
    
    The shared 736 GB english_dclm is the hazard this had to work around.
    variant_dir's `ln -sfn` replaces a regular file, and english_dclm.idx does not
    exist until finalize(), so a link created during the ~15h a build takes would
    have finalize() write straight through it onto the shared index. The registry
    now carries an owns-English field into launch_builds.sh, variant_dir also
    treats a directory holding an english build of its own as owning its English
    whatever that field says, and it refuses to turn any real file into a link.
    Scheme A's english build command is unchanged, argument for argument.
    
    English BPB stays honest across corpora by excluding the 3,561 held-out
    documents by Common Crawl id (validation.dclm.ids.json, derived once from the
    manifest's own dclm entry) instead of by row position, which only works in the
    corpus the validation set was carved from. The count now prints even when zero,
    and an empty id list is refused rather than silently disabling the exclusion. A
    build records its corpus in <prefix>.plan.json and an english_dclm.source
    sidecar, and a resume against a different --dclm_dir is refused rather than
    appending a second corpus to the same .bin.
    
    data/build_status.sh is new: one line per build, DONE / building / STALLED /
    pending, and for an English build the realized-size check nothing else performs
    (fineweb_source short-circuits at L=1, so undersized_build never sees one).
    
    configs/models.json also drops 24 stale rows for untrained shallow replicates
    and the 1B x3 at L50, removed from the grid earlier and never resynced; all 24
    verified to have no checkpoints and no eval results.
    
    Verified: shared english_dclm byte-intact (736,000,002,892 B, mtime unchanged,
    not a symlink) with both builds running; DCLMP/ and FWEB/ hold no english
    symlink; the flat discovery branch returns the same 3,530 files in the same
    order; scheme A's already-built path does not trip the new sidecar guard; a
    pre-change checkpoint still resumes; 185 runs and 314 models recomputed from
    the registry; check_rules 0 findings; both generated doc blocks in sync.

